# Round 12 — the prepare-branch `--main` pass over v0.4.0..HEAD

**Scope:** the full Wave-5 range `v0.4.0..63ac93c` (23 commits, 76
files) — D19 (SaaS adapters + api-client), LP1 (study pack), CLI1-3,
D4/D5/D8/D9/D10/D11 legs, and rounds 10-11's fixes. Engine: the
deep-pr-review pipeline (nine lenses: Correctness, Security, Tests,
Migrations→wire/schema evolution, Performance, Architecture/Packaging,
Product/AC, Cleanup, Hazard-class enumeration with all three triggers
fired) plus one Codex adversarial pass. 54 candidate findings, 34
unverified questions; every actionable finding verified against the
code before fixing. **25 verified findings fixed in one commit; 1 new
deferral recorded; 6 unverified questions carried.**

## Blockers fixed

**R12-1 — the next release would have failed mid-run.** Published
`exocortex-client` depended on `publish = false` `exocortex-api-client`
(crates/exocortex-client/Cargo.toml:33 vs api-client's manifest) — the
registry can never resolve that dependency, and `scripts/publish.sh`
would have discovered it after earlier crates already shipped. Found
independently by two lenses. Fix: api-client is publishable with an
ORDER entry before client; a new publish.sh guard refuses an ORDER
crate whose regular deps name a workspace member not published earlier
(with a disposable fixture scenario); the two SaaS adapter binaries are
now explicitly `publish = false` leaves (the D19 row's own words);
PUBLISHING.md records all of it.

**R12-2 — the SDK silently lost rows a window could not split.**
`split_unit` packed by bytes only; the server rejects a batch over 64
edges / 256 memories with `ResourceLimitExceeded` — a PERMANENT
rejection, so the window settled and the cursor advanced past the lost
rows (exit 2, rows unrecoverable). A git merge commit with 65+ files,
a Linear chunk with 65+ same-project issues, or four full-closing-ref
PRs in one window each hit it. Fix: the split enforces both count
ceilings during packing and refuses a single over-ceiling component
BEFORE the wire (`Unsplittable` now names the bound); the testing mock
enforces the same ceilings so the gap can't go dark again. Tests:
`batches_respect_the_per_batch_count_ceilings`,
`a_component_over_an_edge_ceiling_is_unsplittable_before_the_wire`.

**R12-3 — a fetch-bounded GitHub run stranded un-fetched rows
forever.** The dual-stream shared cursor advanced past rows a walk
never fetched: issues truncated at the bound excluded every un-fetched
NEWER issue from all later runs' `since` filter; pulls truncated
before the backlog stranded the un-walked middle behind the new cursor
— both exiting 0 with "re-run to continue", which was false. Confirmed
independently by a lens and the Codex pass. Fix: per-stream truncation
tracking plus `safe_cursor_ceiling` (issues hold at the fetched
frontier; an un-backlogged pulls walk holds at the resume point; the
written cursor is clamped and the log says why and names the remedy).
Tests: `truncated_runs_hold_the_cursor_below_unfetched_rows`.

**R12-4 — reindex overwrote concurrent writes and could resurrect
retired rows.** `reindex_chunk` wrote the whole scanned row back with
no version precondition (Codex P1): a row updated between scan and
commit was reverted to the scan's content; a retired row was
resurrected. Fix: optimistic concurrency — each changed row is
re-read before commit and skipped when its LSN moved (or the row is
gone), reported as the new `superseded` count through
`ReindexReport`/`ReindexStats`/`ReindexEmbeddingsOutput` (goldens
regenerated). Test: `reindex_skips_rows_superseded_after_the_scan`
(a mutating embedder lands a newer assertion mid-chunk; the stale scan
never wins).

**R12-5 — a cross-repo closing reference overwrote local identity.**
`closingIssuesReferences` can name issues in OTHER repositories, but
closing rows were keyed `issue:{number}` under the TARGET repo's table
uuid — a foreign repo's #10 collided with (and overwrote/deduped
against) local #10, attaching the closing edge to the wrong row (Codex
P1). Fix: the query fetches `repository { nameWithOwner }`, foreign
refs carry composite identity (`issue:{owner/name}#{number}`), the
chunker dedupes on (repo, number), and same-repo refs fold onto the
local identity. Tests:
`foreign_closing_references_keep_their_own_identity`,
`normalize_closing_repos_folds_same_repo_refs`.

**R12-6 — every Dreams cycle rewrote its committed abstraction rows,
and rollback deleted them.** The idempotency guard consulted the
region-typed working set, which can never contain the General-carried
abstraction row — so the guard never fired in a typed region: the full
creation path re-ran each cycle (fresh stamps, perpetual churn,
metric inflation), and the journaled `create_memory` over a
preimage-holding row made any later failure in the cycle physically
DELETE the prior cycle's committed abstraction. Fix: idempotency
consults storage (`get_memory` by the deterministic id). Test: the
existing idempotency test now pins that cycle 2 does not restamp the
committed row (it could not detect the rewrite before).

## Advisories fixed

**R12-7 — cross-domain discovery was non-deterministic and cloned the
region.** The cap took the first 64 of a HashMap iteration (a random
per-process subset — different proposals every cycle, contradicting
the documented determinism and disagreeing with the sorted standalone
path), after deep-cloning the whole working set to use 64 rows; the
pairing pass also lacked the computed-row filter `select_anchors`
applies, so abstraction rows (which inherit member entities) could
pair into CrossDomain proposals. Fix: borrow, sort by id, filter
`Provenance::Computed`, then cap. Tests:
`cross_domain_proposals_skip_computed_rows`; determinism follows from
the ordering (same input ⇒ same subset, both paths).

**R12-8 — the git adapter livelocked on wide commits and buffered
unbounded history.** No `--max-window ≥ 2` parse guard (R10-4's class,
fixed for the SaaS siblings only); the window builder never flushed an
oversize single commit, so a 300-file merge died at
`ProjectionBoundExceeded` on every run; `git log` output (whole
history) was buffered with no bound; the `--range` regression test
could not fail (its range end equaled the value the sequential run had
already written); the exit-2 contract had no test. Fix:
`map_history_bounded` bounds units by rows AND the 64-edge ceiling,
slicing wide commits across units (each slice repeats the commit row
with full content; the frontier advances only past complete commits);
`--max-commits` bounds the read (oldest first; loud continuation);
tests: `bounded_units_respect_the_edge_ceiling`,
`bounded_frontier_advances_only_past_complete_commits`, the corrected
range test (`HEAD~2..HEAD~1`), `the_operator_cursor_is_the_newest_settled_sha`,
`permanent_rejections_exit_nonzero`, `max_window_below_two_is_rejected_at_parse`.

**R12-9 — api-client: untimed bodies, per-request TLS, silent
plaintext, token echo.** The 60s timeout ended at the headers (a
trickling-body endpoint hung the adapter forever, contradicting the
documented "connect + headers + body"); every request built a new
client AND loaded the platform trust store; a non-local `http://`
endpoint sent the bearer cleartext with no warning; error summaries
embedded 500 chars of raw response body with the request token
unredacted. Fix: one deadline over request+body; the client (and its
bounded-retry trust-store load) is built once; non-local plaintext
draws a loud warning; `summarize` redacts the token. Test:
`summaries_redact_the_bearer_token`. (A live body-stall test is not
practical under the constant 60s timeout; the deadline structure is
the fix and the redaction is pinned.)

**R12-10 — SimilarTo hints leaked scoped titles across producers.**
The hint path returned `existing_title` with an org/model/dims filter
only, while the ring stored exactly the visibility needed to gate —
and the sibling D5 seeding path gates by endpoint visibility. A
Private row's title reached a different producer's ack. Fix:
`hint_visible` mirrors `memory_visible`'s scope rules (Private → same
author, scoped Project/Team → same scope, Org+ → anyone; scope-less
Project/Team rows keep the D10c advisory contract; an authorless
Private row hints nobody). Test: `hints_respect_row_visibility_scope`.

**R12-11 — similarity seeding held the ring mutex across
O(rows×ring×dims) cosines**, serializing concurrent submits behind one
opt-in loop. Fix: snapshot the ring under the lock, compute outside.

**R12-12 — `chunk_windows` rebuilt the per-window dedupe set per
group** (O(window²) inserts; pathological at operator-raised
`--max-window`). Fix: maintained incrementally (now keyed by
(repo, number) for R12-5).

**R12-13 — adapters buffered full raw bodies pre-chunking** (bounded
only by the 16 MiB page cap). Fix: `bound_4000` applies at parse time
(map time never needed more).

**R12-14 — the exhaustive kernel↔wire ProducerKind pin was gone**
(round 10 deleted the kernel-crate test; only arms 6/7 were pinned; a
renumber of arms 0-5 passed every gate). Fix: an exhaustive ingest test
over all 8 values plus fail-closed 8+; the server policy-table loop now
pins all 7 shipped kinds (its comment claimed "every shipped kind"
while pinning two).

**R12-15 — the rule-9 ledger was stale**: `exocortex-dreams →
exocortex-wire` (D8's canonical digest) and cluster's `prost`
dev-dependency (D10) were unrecorded. Fixed in PUBLISHING.md.

**R12-16 — the kind-7 downgrade blast radius was undocumented**: a
binary rollback across a producer-kind addition fails the whole source
registry read (all ingest down, not just SaaS) because `ProducerKind`
is a serde enum without unknown-variant fallback. The wire path's
fail-closed behavior was blessed; the serde path's posture now is too:
forward-only, stated in PUBLISHING.md.

**R12-17 — cross-pack kind-NAME collisions were silent**: kind ids are
pack-namespaced so the by-id duplicate check cannot fire, and name
resolution (`kind_id`) picks whichever the HashMap yields first. Fix:
the kernel rejects a duplicate kind display name at composition
(`DuplicateKindName`), mapped to a rejection in ingest.

**R12-18 / R12-19 — round-11's operator-doc claims were half-delivered**:
README still enumerated kinds 1-6 (the R11-8 half that never landed);
three operator-facing bail strings still carried rewrap artifacts
(R11V-7's exact class). Both fixed.

**R12-20 — D5's "opt-in" had no operator surface**: the seeding flag
had exactly one caller (its test) — no shipped binary could enable it.
Fix: `EXOCORTEX_INGEST_SIMILARITY_SEEDING=1|true|on|yes` on the backend
node (default off, the recorded lean).

**R12-21 — the three-node fan-out test never touched nodes b/c**: the
name and the D10 row claimed cluster fan-out; only node-a's ring was
exercised. Fix: the publisher's retained window is delivered into both
peers and every node's log is asserted (frontier + LSN order).

**R12-22 — playbook prose contradicted its own generated block** ("The
8 buckets (48 kinds)" beside "72 kinds are declared") and the gen
marker still said "regenerated from exocortex-pack-dev-v1". Fixed in
the artifact AND the generator.

**R12-23 — the CLI rendered the wrong output shapes**: `chain` printed
`? [?]` for every step (GetChainOutput is hex ids, not objects);
`related` probed shapes the registry never emits. Fixed; the smoke
draft fixture no longer races on a shared temp path.

**R12-24 — small correctness-of-record cleanups**: dead
`reindex_embeddings` arm in http_parity's input closure; the stale
`unreachable!("chunks() honors the bound")` (the bound it named was
not the one enforced); the study pack's crate header named the
pre-rename path; the chaos-fixture comment omitted study-v1 from the
composed set; linear's `by_id` map values were never read.

**R12-25 — live-leg skips were invisible in CI**: libtest captures a
passing test's stderr, so the adapters' "UNEXECUTED" lines never
reached the canonical log. Fix: verify-release.sh echoes the GitHub/
Linear leg status at shell level, exactly like the Postgres leg.

## Confirmed deferrals (not re-litigated)

- Adapter-runner extraction with identity-pinning test (R10-18/R11).
- Binary-level cursor tests for the linear/github adapters (R11).
- CLI export passthrough + stats op (CLI3 reconciliation).
- Live legs: FALKOR/REDIS/POSTGRES URLs unset throughout this round;
  the GitHub live leg executed in D19; Linear awaits the owner-minted
  key.

## New deferral (recorded as Backlog rows R12-D1/R12-D2 in the plan)

- **R12-D1 — stale-abstraction retirement.** The R12-6 fix stops the
  rewrite and the rollback deletion, but nothing retires an abstraction
  whose member set has changed: PRUNE iterates the region-typed
  working set (General-carried rows are invisible there), and
  abstraction rows never receive `valid_until`. Retiring superseded
  abstractions needs a design (PRUNE over the carrier type, or
  valid_until stamping when a class re-abstracts) — v2, with this
  round as its evidence.

## Unverified questions (carried, not findings)

- GitHub's `filterBy: {since}` boundary inclusivity is the resume
  design's own premise but has never been confirmed by a live leg
  (R12-3's fix holds the cursor conservatively regardless).
- [unverified] Can Linear's cursor pagination re-emit the same issue
  id within one walk? If yes, the chunker's per-window id dedupe
  (added for github in R11V-1) would matter there too.
- [unverified] Does FalkorDB `stream_all_memories` snapshot before
  streaming? R12-4's LSN recheck makes reindex safe against concurrent
  writes on any backend, but the live scan's revisit behavior under
  mid-scan commits remains unexamined without `FALKOR_URL`.
- The wire_fanout floor assert (5,000/s) is a wall-clock check without
  the bench gate's SLO-multiplier relief — flake risk on loaded
  shared runners is accepted, not verified.
- hyper-rustls sits outside the no-llm outbound-client allowlist (it
  cannot originate requests without hyper); naming it in the allowlist
  is a possible hardening, deliberately not taken this round.
- rust-analyzer reports E0308 at drain.rs:248; the full matrix (clippy
  + workspace tests) adjudicates — if green, it is an LSP
  generic-inference artifact.

## Verify-clauses

Every fix above carries a test or gate that fails without it, named in
its entry; the commit carrying this document is the closing evidence.
The loop is NOT yet at a verified-clean exit: this pass found and
fixed 25, so the iteration-6 full pass is the next gate (the round-11
precedent: keep passing until a pass returns zero).

## Iteration 6 — the second wave (2026-09-08)

The loop continued per its own rule (a pass that finds work is not an
exit). Nine lenses + Codex over the fix commit itself found **14 more
verified findings, all fixed**:

**R12-26 — R12-5 was inert in production.** The closing-refs GraphQL
query never requested `repository { nameWithOwner }` — the edit that
added it was the one failed hunk in iteration 5's batch, and the
synthetic test fixtures supplied the field the query could not
return. Foreign refs parsed `repo: None` and collided with local
identity exactly as before. Fixed (query + tests that fail without
the field), plus case-insensitive same-repo folding (`--owner
Microsoft` vs canonical `microsoft/vscode`) and unsanitized draft
keys (`a-b/c` vs `a/b-c` cannot collide).

**R12-27 — retired SaaS rows were permanently rejected.** A
closed-unmerged PR sets `valid_until = closedAt` (past) with
`valid_from = None`; ingest defaults valid_from to now and rejects
`valid_until < valid_from` (Permanent) — the cursor advanced past
every retired row. Both adapters now fetch `createdAt` and stamp it
as valid_from.

**R12-28 — zero-file commits were dropped** (merges list no paths
under plain `--name-only`): the bounded mapper's slice loop never ran,
the row never emitted, the frontier advanced past it. Fixed
(commit-only slices).

**R12-29 — one commit sliced twice could land twice in one unit**
(shared paths + tight row room): duplicate `commit-{sha}` draft key →
SDK `InvalidUnit` on every retry. A commit's slices never share a
group now (pinned).

**R12-30 — Dreams abstracted across private members.** The
abstraction carried the first member's context under the narrowest
label: a Private-member abstraction readable by member 0's author
named other authors' private titles in its content. Classes now
partition by (visibility, scope subject); mixed-scope classes never
share an abstraction (the dataset test now pins the private member's
exclusion).

**R12-31 — the hint gate's scope-less carve-out contradicted
`memory_visible`.** Scope-less Project/Team rows are unreadable by
every principal, yet hinted everyone — and new scope-less rows are
still creatable. The gate now denies them (aligned with "missing
scope never widens"); the D10c test submits scoped rows and pins the
scope-less exclusion.

**R12-32 — the ring re-retained full title+content text** (512 × 64
KiB potential): `content_exact` is a blake3 digest now. The hint
scan also left the ring mutex (snapshot like the seeding path), and
seeding early-returns when the batch has no embedded rows.

**R12-33 — `--max-commits` bounded the Vec, not the read**: the log
was captured whole. The read is streamed and cut at the bound.

**R12-34 — api-client redaction gaps:** the GraphQL error path joined
server messages unredacted; summarize truncated BEFORE redacting
(boundary-straddling echoes leaked prefixes); `.local` hosts were
exempt from the plaintext-bearer warning. All fixed.

**R12-35 — `Summarizes` renumbered dev-v1 kind ids** (D8 inserted it
mid-list; `Teaches` and everything after shifted a local id — the
opposite of a superset for stored RelKindIds). Moved to the END; the
authorized fingerprint move `9f92957f… → a427b3ad…` (golden + study
literal + AGENTS/CLAUDE/plan updated in-commit).

**R12-36 — the CLI write path:** `add` exited 0 on rejected writes
(exit 3 now) and hardcoded the `cli` project with no team surface
(`EXOCORTEX_PROJECT`/`EXOCORTEX_TEAM` honored).

**R12-37 — verify-release's live-leg echoes were one-sided:** with a
token set the legs never ran and nothing said so. They run now
(matching the Postgres leg's contract).

**R12-38 — guard/doc gaps:** publish guard covers build-deps
(+fixture scenario); git adapter carries `publish = false` like its
siblings; PUBLISHING's manual order listed ingest before dreams and
omitted study; README documents the D5 and CLI env vars; the kernel
entry-point doc lists all five failure modes; `DuplicateKindName`
has its fail-without-it test; the SDK memory ceiling is pinned at the
PACKING level (a >256-memory welded component is unreachable while
the edge ceiling is tighter — a component of N memories carries >=
N-1 edges — so the edge arm provably fires first; the invariant is
stated at the arm).

**R12-39 — `map_history` retired:** the unbounded single-unit mapper
was production-dead after R12-8 (test-only); deleted, E2E tests ported
to the bounded path, the stale doc link and the duplicated WHY block
removed.

**New deferral: R12-D2** — reindex's LSN recheck is check-then-act; a
write landing between recheck and commit is still overwritten. The
real fix is an LSN-preconditioned (conditional) upsert at the storage
seam — recorded in the plan with the other v2 items.

**Not fixed, recorded:** github/linear binary exit-2 legs need a dual
mock harness (GraphQL + backend) — folded into the binary-cursor-tests
deferral. Carried unverified: GitHub `since` inclusivity (live);
Linear same-id re-emission; Falkor stream snapshot semantics;
wire_fanout wall-clock floor flake posture; hyper-rustls allowlist
naming; drain.rs:248 rust-analyzer report (matrix green = artifact).

## Iteration 7 — the third wave (2026-09-19)

**Scope:** the release range `12c8825..2d90018` (15 commits, 18 files)
— the 0.4.1 bump, REL1's compile sweep + four rotted suites, REL3's
publish guard, D30's thread-scoped allocator, D31's standalone project
grant, and D32's rustls bump; the loop's first pass over the work that
shipped v0.4.1. Engine: the deep-pr-review pipeline with EIGHT lenses +
one Codex adversarial pass (clean: "no actionable regressions"); no
hazard-class triggers fired (the D31 grant is an idempotent one-path
set-insert, no new data kind or destination moved, the boot WAL scan is
one-shot local). 22 candidate findings consolidated to 12 actionable
(all advisory-grade; the AC table for all six range items came back
Met), 2 retired by design, 5 observations recorded; every fix carries
its evidence below.

**Fixed:**

**R12-40 — the publish guard's strip set was a second hand-synced
literal.** `publish.sh` carried `needs_strip()`'s membership twice (the
python exemption set and the shell case); a crate added to one but not
the other re-opened exactly the REL3 mid-release failure the guard
exists to stop (fail-open direction). One `STRIP_CRATES` array now
feeds both, and the two python guard blocks merged into one (the
duplicated prologue goes with it). `bash scripts/tests/publish.sh`
green; the fixture's existing gregs pin both refusal strings.

**R12-41 — REL1's six-feature sweep was unpinned against drift.** A
seventh crate declaring `integration` would rott invisibly behind its
gate again — the exact REL1 class. `cargo xtask deployment-acceptance`
now derives the feature-declaring crates from the workspace manifests
and refuses unless verify-release.sh compiles each one under
`--no-run`; `integration_sweep_pins_every_declaring_crate` fails
against a doctored sweep (missing crate, dropped --no-run).

**R12-42 — the alloc gate had no positive control.** Both window tests
assert deltas of zero; a broken hook (increment dropped, allocator
unhooked) left the gate permanently green while measuring nothing.
`same_thread_allocations_do_count_in_the_window` pins 64 same-thread
allocations land in the window exactly.

**R12-43 — alloc.rs prose was wrong and bloated.** The struct doc said
`dealloc` "needs no arm" beside its required-trait arm; the module doc
restated the D30 row. Both cut to accurate 2-3 line forms; the
PRD-naming divergence (stats_alloc/dhat vs the hand-rolled allocator)
is now deviation 22 in MILESTONE_REPORT (rule 8's ledger, not a PRD
edit).

**R12-44 — the online end_session path never granted the batch's
project** (the D31 bug class, backend half): the server acked, the
reseed delivered, and the client's own read filter dropped the row
forever. The ack now grants the batch's project into the session scope
(mirrors the offline fix; only widens the LOCAL filter for rows the
server already delivered). Fail-without-it:
`backend_project_grant.rs::backend_project_visibility_write_is_searchable_after_ack`
(in-process backend node, principal carrying the project so delivery
is proven, poll to the served hit; red with the grant stashed, green
fixed). The SERVER half — principal project membership for SSE
delivery — stays PLT1's recorded concern, now noted on its row.

**R12-45 — preflight_wrapup ignored its project_id.** A proposed
batch's cross-batch edge targets in the batch's own project did not
resolve (the lookup scope excluded it — the very widening end_session's
self-preflight applies). The batch's project now joins the lookup scope
only.

**R12-46 — dead dedup + O(M×P) boot scan in main.rs.** The boot join's
`.any()` guard was provably dead (the base vc is empty two lines up)
and the accumulating dedupe was a Vec::contains scan; the wal project
set is a HashSet assigned once.

**R12-47 — PUBLISHING.md contradicted itself about 0.4.1.** Four
"Unreleased" blocks described crates the same file recorded as shipped;
the publish-script contract paragraph omitted the new dev-dep refusal
(and needs_strip); the supported-entry-point example still said
`PUBLISH_VERSION=0.4.0` against a 0.4.1 workspace. All reconciled; the
four 0.4.1 records relabelled "Shipped in 0.4.1".

**R12-48 — D32's lockfile bump carried unexplained collateral.** The
`cargo update --precise` re-resolution also re-pointed tempfile's
getrandom edge 0.4.3 → 0.3.4 (its requirement admits both; deny
advisories stay green; no bounded restore exists — `cargo update -p
tempfile` changes nothing). Recorded here and on the D32 row rather
than hand-edited into the lock.

**R12-49 — the D32 row misstated the rustls topology and lacked its
matrix evidence.** Per the lock, `exocortex-client` and
`exocortex-api-client` ride rustls **0.21.12** (hyper-rustls 0.24
stack), not 0.23.45 — the 0.23 line belongs to the server path — and
the rerun's green is CI run 34947142714 (2026-09-15). Row corrected;
the EOL 0.21 line itself is new Backlog row D33.

**R12-50 — four D1 adapter manifests could publish by mistake.**
parquet/iceberg/delta/postgres were documented never-ship but left
publishable (linear/github carry `publish = false`); all four now do,
with the PUBLISHING record.

**R12-51 — github_live's mapper leg skipped silently on an empty
window** (a green run that never mapped). The skip is loud and names
the remedy, matching the repo's loud-skip discipline.

**Retired (not defects):** a standalone "foreign project stays hidden"
wiring test is unfulfillable by design — standalone grants every WAL
project (single-principal contract, main.rs) and the cache layer
already pins `memory_visible`'s exclusion; target-specific dev-deps DO
carry `kind: "dev"` in cargo metadata (verified empirically), so the
REL3 guard sees them; CI publishes only through the fixture-tested
scripts (no workspace publish surface).

**Recorded observations:** per-read `effective_vc` cost is O(P) in
granted projects (P is the harness's stable project ids in practice;
PLT1 produces the real shape); boot seeding grants all WAL projects
regardless of author (the local-file trust boundary is unchanged — a
WAL writer could always plant readable org rows); cdc_live passes the
DSN in psql argv (pre-existing, gated). Carried unverified from the
second wave, unchanged: GitHub `since` inclusivity; Linear same-id
re-emission; Falkor stream snapshot semantics; wire_fanout wall-clock
posture; hyper-rustls allowlist naming; drain.rs:248 analyzer report.

## Iteration 8 — the loop over iteration 7's fixes (2026-09-19)

**Scope:** the iteration-7 fix commits `2d90018..6a69a23` (11 code
files). Engine: eight lenses + one Codex pass (clean again); no hazard
triggers. 20 candidates consolidated to 4 actionable (one found
independently by five lenses), several verified-clean notes, and the
carried unverified list unchanged.

**Fixed:**

**R12-52 — the sweep pin was weaker than its purpose** (Correctness,
Tests, Wire/schema, Architecture, Cleanup — independent). The
substring-anywhere checks could be satisfied from the token-gated LIVE
legs (`-p exocortex-adapter-github` appears there too), the `--no-run`
check was disjoint from the per-crate check, and the feature detector
matched one canonical spelling (`integration=[]` would escape —
fail-open). `validate_integration_sweep` now anchors to the actual
`--no-run` command (from its `cargo test` to the flag), parses its
`-p` and `--features` tokens, and enforces a TWO-WAY bijection with
the manifest-derived declaring set (a stale sweep entry also refuses);
`declares_integration` accepts any TOML spacing. Tests pin each
direction: gutted sweep, stale entry, dropped `--no-run`, spacing
variants.

**R12-53 — the online grant fired on a rejected ack** (Correctness +
Security). `tool.handle` returns `Ok` with `accepted: 0` when local
preflight rejects every draft, so a failed batch still widened the
session read scope for the process lifetime. The grant now fires only
on `accepted > 0`. Fail-without-it:
`a_rejected_batch_does_not_grant_its_project` — a second client writes
an other-project row, the reader (hydrated with it at boot) submits an
invalid batch in that project and must NOT see the row (red with the
guard weakened to `if true`, green fixed).

**R12-54 — the manifest walker aborted the gate on non-crate
entries** (Correctness + Wire/schema). Any stray file or manifest-less
directory under `crates/` killed deployment-acceptance with an opaque
io error; such entries now skip (they carry no gated suites).

**R12-55 — harness and prose hardening** (Cleanup + Tests). The e2e
reads through the shared `support::BoundedLineReader` (byte-bounded;
`read_json_id` added) instead of a hand-rolled unbounded channel pump —
also fixing the timeout-misread-as-stdout-closed diagnostic; the grant
comment and the test module doc cut to their WHY.

**Recorded, not fixed:** the merged publish guard reports whichever
violation comes first in ORDER when a crate has both kinds (cosmetic;
both refuse pre-mutation — the fixture's own reset comment
acknowledges the interleave); the ~100-line backend-harness duplication
across the two e2e files is the repo's established per-file idiom
(extract at the third backend harness, per the standalone_readback /
backup_restore precedent); performance re-examined `effective_vc` and
confirmed the diff only POPULATES the recorded O(P) shape in backend
mode (G ≤ distinct written projects, SmallVec-bounded) and improves
boot to O(M). The fixture's study strip-then-publish leg remains
unexecuted by construction (the refusal aborts before publish_one) —
covered instead by the single-sourced STRIP_CRATES, cluster's
strip/restore leg, and the guard-exemption scenario. Retired: the
test-vs-gate split for the sweep (the literal six in the unit test
cannot go stale into a false pass — the gate derives from manifests);
commit-hash and CI-run questions (confirmed by the run log).

**Carried unverified, unchanged:** GitHub `since` inclusivity; Linear
same-id re-emission; Falkor stream snapshot semantics; wire_fanout
wall-clock posture; hyper-rustls allowlist naming; drain.rs:248
